<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santander, Spain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santander is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>. A medieval fishing and trading town, it grew in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>'s port for wool and the colonial trade. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it fell to Nationalist and Italian forces in August 1937, isolating the remaining Republican north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santander-spain.jpg" alt="Santander, Spain" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noreenlam · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Palacio_de_la_Magdalena_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Santander lies on the northern side of the Bay of Santander, facing the Cantabrian Sea, and owes its existence to the excellent harbour of the bay. The earliest settlers probably chose the north side for practical reasons: it is sheltered from the storms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> and has a hillside with good visibility for spotting attackers. Little is known of the earliest settlements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place is first mentioned in 1068, in a draft document of King Sancho II. According to the account, Alfonso II the Chaste had founded the Abbey of the Holy Bodies in the 9th century, to house the heads of Saint Emeterius and Saint Celedonius. Alfonso VIII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> granted a fuero, or charter, in 1187. The shipyards were ordered by the king to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from the Cantabrian forests, and the town had to give the monarchy a ship each year. By the end of the 15th century it had about 4,000 or 5,000 inhabitants. It became a city officially in 1755.</p><p>On 3 November 1893 the steamship Cabo Machichaco caught fire while being unloaded at a pier. About 40 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">dynamite</a> in her holds exploded, killing about 590 people, injuring about 2,000 and destroying at least 65 buildings near the harbour.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> the Nationalist uprising of 1936 in Santander was defeated, and the city stayed with the Republican government. Nationalist forces captured it in the Battle of Santander on 26 August 1937. Adam Hoschild counts at least 1,267 people executed after quick trials in the weeks that followed. In 1941 a great fire burned for two days, destroyed the greater part of the medieval town centre and gutted the Romanesque cathedral. Only one person died, a firefighter, but thousands of families were left homeless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Santander is the capital of the autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>. The cathedral keeps its lower church, built around 1200 in a style passing from Romanesque to Gothic. The port remains active, with ferry services to the United Kingdom and Ireland, and the bank Banco Santander was founded in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santander, Spain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Bilbao fell to Franco in June 1937, and as <b>Santander</b> fell in late August, Asturias and part of León were isolated from the Republican government in Valencia.</blockquote><a href="/state/sovereign-council-of-asturias-and-leon">Sovereign Council of Asturias and León</a></li></ul></section>
